# Normalise all text to LF, in the repository and in every working tree.
#
# Why this file exists — it was added by the M1 independent verification pass,
# after `pnpm format:check` and `pnpm verify` were found to exit 1 on Windows.
#
# The failure: `.prettierrc.json` pins `"endOfLine": "lf"`, but with no
# `.gitattributes`, the working-tree line ending was left to each contributor's
# `core.autocrlf`. Stock Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` (set in
# `C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig`, not by any local override), so a Windows
# clone checked files out as CRLF and Prettier rejected all 34 governed files.
# `pnpm verify` exited 1 while CI stayed green, because `autocrlf` is inert on
# Linux and the runner checked out LF.
#
# That is the worst kind of gate defect: a documented, committed, false result.
# CI was green three times and the failure was still invisible, because every
# gate that *executes* code passed. Only reading the docs against the declared
# supported environment surfaced it.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working-tree line ending a committed fact rather than a
# function of the host OS, so a green CI run and a green local run finally mean
# the same thing. It also overrides a contributor's local `core.autocrlf` for
# tracked files, so a misconfigured editor can no longer silently diverge from
# CI.
#
# Do not remove this file on the belief that "git handles line endings". It does
# not, unless it is told to, and the repository does tell it here.
#
# Everything is already stored as LF in the index, so this file changes no
# repository content. It only changes how the working tree is materialised.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets, if any are ever added. The repository currently contains none,
# and `text=auto` already content-sniffs, so these are belt-and-braces against
# the well-known `text=auto` failure mode of misclassifying a binary as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary

# The M0 spike keeps its own lockfile and is not a workspace member. Its own
# tooling is hand-rolled; declaring it here keeps that intent explicit even
# though nothing currently requires an exception.
tests/provider-spike/** text=auto eol=lf